This article summarizes how platformOS protects your applications and data, and how it recovers from failures. Policies, audit reports and detailed security documentation are available in the [platformOS Trust Center](https://trust.platformos.com/).

## Hosting

platformOS runs on managed Kubernetes. By default, we recommend Oracle Cloud Infrastructure (OCI), but platformOS can be deployed on any major cloud provider, such as Amazon Web Services (AWS), Google Cloud or Microsoft Azure, for example when a client has contractual obligations to use a specific provider.

Applications run either on the shared platform or on a **dedicated private stack**, where the cluster, network, databases, search, secrets store and file storage are reserved for a single client. Both are multi-tenant: a private stack can host any number of the client's instances, and on either platform each instance is isolated from the others at the application level.

## Network and traffic

* Public traffic passes through Cloudflare, which provides DNS, TLS and DDoS mitigation. A web application firewall can be enabled per stack.
* Traffic is distributed by the load balancing service of the chosen cloud provider to the ingress layer, which terminates TLS, redirects HTTP to HTTPS, sets HSTS and other security headers, and applies rate limits.
* Databases, cache and search have no public endpoints and are reachable only from inside the cluster.
* Administrative access to the infrastructure requires an individually named identity with multi-factor authentication.

## Data protection

* **In transit:** all traffic is served over HTTPS.
* **At rest:** disks, databases, file storage and backups are encrypted by the infrastructure provider. Sensitive values, such as constants and integration credentials, are additionally encrypted at the application level.
* **Passwords:** stored as bcrypt hashes. Applications can enable two-factor authentication with time-based one-time passwords (TOTP) for their users.
* **File uploads:** uploads use signed, short-lived authorizations that confine each file to the instance that issued it. Allowed file types and maximum file sizes are enforced on upload, and private files are only accessible through signed URLs that expire.

## Backups

platformOS operates its own PostgreSQL clusters and backs up the platform database to three independent locations:

| Copy | Schedule | Default retention |
|---|---|---|
| Inside the cluster | Weekly full backup and continuous transaction log archiving | 7 weekly backups |
| Off-cluster object storage | Weekly full backup, daily incremental backups and continuous transaction log archiving | 8 weekly backups |
| A second cloud provider | Daily database dump | 30 days, write-protected |

Continuous transaction log archiving allows the database to be restored to any point in time within the retention window, with at most about one minute of data loss. Backup jobs are monitored, and a failed or overdue backup raises an alert to the on-call team. Search indexes are rebuilt from the database and are not backed up separately. Backup schedules and retention for dedicated private stacks can be agreed per engagement.

