## High availability

* The database runs as a cluster with a primary instance, read replicas and automatic failover.
* Application servers scale automatically with load.
* The entire infrastructure is defined as code and can be rebuilt from version control.

## Disaster recovery

The Disaster Recovery Plan (DRP) defines the following recovery objectives:

| Failure | Recovery time objective (RTO) | Recovery point objective (RPO) |
|---|---|---|
| Database primary failure | About 1 minute (automatic failover) | About 1 minute |
| Data corruption or an erroneous change | Under 30 minutes (point-in-time restore) | About 1 minute |
| Loss of the cluster | 24 hours | About 1 minute |
| Loss of the cloud region | 24 hours | Up to 24 hours |

### Restoration testing

Backup restoration is tested quarterly, and after any change to the backup setup. Each test:

1. Restores the off-cluster backup into a new, isolated database cluster, without affecting production.
2. Recovers it to a defined point in time.
3. Compares every table row by row against a snapshot of production taken for that point.
4. Checks data integrity, indexes and schema, and confirms the restored data is accessible through the application.

Recent tests restored the production database to the requested point in time well within the recovery time objective, with no data loss and no differences from production.

Current service status is published at [status.platformos.dev](https://status.platformos.dev).
